WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.873
A valid API key should not automatically mean full access.

2
00:00:03.873 --> 00:00:08.749
Authentication establishes that the request presented a known credential.

3
00:00:08.749 --> 00:00:12.756
Authorization decides what that credential is allowed to do.

4
00:00:12.756 --> 00:00:16.362
Keeping those decisions separate is essential for ASP.

5
00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:22.172
NET Core APIs that support multiple clients, tenants, environments, or operation types.

6
00:00:22.172 --> 00:00:28.183
API keys should be scoped to a purpose, environment, tenant, client, or allowed operation.

7
00:00:28.183 --> 00:00:33.125
A reporting integration may need read access to a narrow set of endpoints.

8
00:00:33.125 --> 00:00:37.933
A deployment automation key may need a specific administrative function.

9
00:00:37.933 --> 00:00:40.805
A partner key may need one tenant boundary.

10
00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:44.211
Broad keys should be rare, approved, and monitored.

11
00:00:44.211 --> 00:00:50.155
Server-side authorization must enforce endpoint, method, resource, and tenant boundaries.

12
00:00:50.155 --> 00:00:57.033
A client-supplied tenant identifier should be checked against trusted key metadata or server-side data.

13
00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:04.513
The caller should not be able to choose a tenant or privilege boundary merely by sending a value in the request.

14
00:01:04.513 --> 00:01:12.126
Key metadata can help decide allowed operations, rate limits, data boundaries, expiration, owner, and review path.

15
00:01:12.126 --> 00:01:21.409
That metadata should be managed as part of the key lifecycle and used consistently by authorization logic, monitoring, and support tooling.

16
00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:29.490
Administrative, export, payment, configuration, destructive, and high-cost operations need explicit authorization review.

17
00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:35.434
These operations can have broad business impact, create cost, or change production state.

18
00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:39.508
They should not be covered only by a generic valid-key check.

19
00:01:39.508 --> 00:01:45.118
API keys may identify an application or integration rather than an individual human.

20
00:01:45.118 --> 00:01:48.123
That affects accountability and audit design.

21
00:01:48.123 --> 00:01:58.741
Logs and records should make it clear which key, owner, client, tenant, or integration performed an action and what human approval path applies where required.
